# Changelog

All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.

## Unreleased

### Removed

- `auto_reach_agent` and `zero_agent_mesure` CLI commands and scripts.

### Added

- State-machine learning lecture with real-to-sim motion comparisons, an example
  agent prompt, and starting-pose, grasp-height, and pickup-timing lessons.

- Consolidated simulation practice and recording into Simulation Teleoperation,
  after Domain Randomization, retaining collection planning and optional scripted collection.

- Expanded-DR teleoperation, evaluation, and state-machine tasks retain DR
  appearance while sampling left/center/right racks and opposite-side floor
  vials. XY sampling areas expand by 20%, rack centers remain at least 33 cm
  from the base, and layouts require a clear reachable grasp/slot pair.
  Exhausted layout searches reject only the affected environment's episode.


### Fixed

- Real-robot DAgger accepts `--start_pose_dataset` and reuses evaluation's
  mean episode-start selection for both arms' reset targets.

- Start-pose analysis reads complete local metadata and Parquet data without
  contacting the Hub, including datasets downloaded without videos.

- Workshop real-rollout hotkeys read only from the focused rollout terminal,
  preventing typing in other applications from starting or scoring trials.
- Learning-content commands match the Newton container launch and current
  evaluation scoring. Dataset upload examples now include LeRobot version tags,
  and the E2E real rollout defines its task instruction.

- Newton uses SAH mesh BVH construction for both implicit and explicit presets
  to avoid the reproduced cuBQL mesh-overlap CUDA fault in the pinned runtime.
  CUDA graphs remain enabled.

- Scripted vial insertion and placement checks follow the selected hole when
  the rack moves. Carrying targets follow that hole until release is anchored;
  release holds the fixed finger at its anchor before the open-jaw return.
- Scripted arm IK accounts for joint bounds when distributing pose error among
  joints, instead of relying on clipping the integrated target. Velocity and
  acceleration limits remain in effect; infeasible placements still time out.

- Simulation evaluation applies camera renaming to outgoing images and feature
  descriptions before server-side resizing, allowing checkpoints with different
  camera names (such as Johnny42's `front`) to use `--rename_map`.


### Changed

- Workshop rollout loads the policy once per session and resets inference state
  between trials instead of reloading checkpoint weights after S/F/Right/N.
  Live camera updates remain available while paused.

- Rename the workshop `lerobot-rollout-v2` command to `lerobot-rollout`,
  replacing the upstream executable when the workshop is installed after LeRobot.

- **Breaking:** base vial assets directly include the foam, paper, and lightbox
  material appearance; DR owns grouped material variation and the perception
  lighting ranges. Regenerate base/DR datasets and retrain affected policies.
- **Breaking:** base vial tasks include per-camera grading and explicit
  `tonemap:operator = "none"`, bypassing OVRtx's default ACES approximation.
  DR adds film grain and episode-fixed wrist blur (sigma 0.7–1.1 pixels) for
  teleoperation, evaluation, and scripted collection. Vision-DR and Perception-DR task IDs are removed;
  select the regular DR IDs. Regenerate datasets and retrain policies collected
  with native DR camera processing. The shared configurable
  `SO101OVRTXRenderer` keeps camera processing configurable.
- Wrist lens and housing corrections are authored directly in the workshop
  robot USD. Material override layers and `configure_perception_scene` are removed.

- Sim evaluation and workshop real rollout accept optional `--start_pose_dataset`; their
  reset pose is the average first-frame joint state across all dataset episodes.
  Missing data is fetched without videos. The scripted start
  pose lives in the state-machine configuration. Omitting the dataset logs a
  warning and uses that scripted pose.

- Perception-DR wrist blur samples sigma from 0.7–1.1 pixels instead of
  1.1–1.8, centered on 0.9 pixels. Applies to teleop, scripted collection,
  and evaluation; this changes their recorded and policy-input image distribution.

- The workshop vial asset directly authors SDF collisions for its body and the
  supplied beveled cap mesh; its primitive-derived mass distribution is fixed.
  The workshop SO-101 override directly authors SDF collisions for the two
  detailed claw meshes. Visible meshes and camera corrections are preserved.
  Collision dynamics change; regenerate demonstrations for this
  experimental configuration.

- Newton/MJWarp contact capacity is 1024.

- Vial static and dynamic friction are 0.20 and normal-contact `solref` is
  `(0.008, 1.0)` for the candidate release-contact response.
  Release improvement is unproven; regenerate demonstrations for these dynamics.

- **Breaking:** scripted vial collection generates procedural paths from the
  actual robot pose, vial geometry, and live rack slot instead of selecting a
  stored demonstration trajectory. Seven phases use pooled timing, speed, grasp,
  and opening measurements from all ten `sreetz-nv/so101_real_manual` episodes at
  revision `975f14991f6d944f8ae418512998a66a0151e40a`. Continuous parameter sampling
  and a shared pace factor vary attempts. Regenerate scripted datasets and
  discard staging artifacts from earlier controllers.
- A 30-frame settling period holds the actual starting pose; reach includes all
  initial translation and wrist reorientation. Approach opening is sampled at
  28–43%, and all procedural opening commands are capped at calibrated 50%.
  Targets follow reachable vial and slot positions, including an upright rack's
  yaw, with no fixed left/right transfer direction.
- Procedural reach elevation follows the measured grasp-radius correlation.
  Carry uses 30–55 mm clearance above insertion, a small inward curve to preserve
  elbow bend, and measured tip-clearance gating. Approach screening includes the
  rack and upright vials. World-up projection handles randomized robot-base tilt.
- Scripted arm control uses a coupled five-joint solve for the jaw midpoint and
  gripper's body-Y direction, bounded resolved-rate target integration, and
  coordinated arm velocity and acceleration limits. The 0.20-rad target-lead bound
  retains servo preload without allowing unbounded integration against contact.
  Opening compensates the midpoint shift caused by the moving jaw while holding
  the fixed finger near its release anchor. Contact gates confirm acquisition,
  lift, tip engagement, seating, and clearance before return; acceptance requires
  a valid upright placement through the completed return.
- The internal motion action has seven components; recorded LeRobot actions
  retain the canonical six joint targets, calibration, and observation timing.
  Controller changes preserve the task's geometry, camera, rendering, actuator,
  and contact configuration. The ten demonstrations supply motion priors;
  model-derived poses and visual grasp estimates do not establish reliability
  across unobserved layouts.
- Non-finite state, Jacobian, or commands in scripted arm IK produce diagnostic
  warnings, finite held targets, and isolated environment timeout resets.

- The workshop wrist camera uses a visually fitted OpenCV fisheye lens and
  camera-to-gripper transform, composed through a local USD correction layer.
  The camera housing follows the same rigid mount correction. This changes
  wrist images in collection and evaluation; regenerate datasets for this camera
  configuration and keep training and evaluation on the same projection. The
  fit is specific to the workshop camera and is not a validated calibration for
  other robots.

- Scripted release targets 0.52 rad instead of 0.65 rad, with opening speed
  increased from 1.2 to 1.5 rad/s and acceleration from 4.0 to 5.0 rad/s².
  Regenerate demonstrations for the narrower, faster release.

- Scripted collection ends successful episodes 1.75 seconds after retraction
  starts instead of waiting for the arm and gripper to reach home. Placement
  confirmation and pulled-out-vial rejection still apply. This shortens recorded
  episode tails; regenerate demonstrations to use the new ending.

### Added
- `lerobot_dagger`: HG-DAgger collection in simulation. A LeRobot policy drives
  one Newton environment; the operator pauses, aligns the physical SO-101 leader
  to the simulated arm, corrects, and hands back. Each correction is saved as
  one LeRobot episode, encoded while recording in a separate writer process.
  `--real` runs the same session on the physical follower; `--leader_follows`
  makes the leader track the arm between corrections; `--demo_video` saves the
  session as one video labelled with who controls the arm. The browser page
  colours its control panel by who drives the arm and lists its keys in
  Workflow and Safety columns, dimming keys the current phase ignores.
  See `DAGGER.md`.
- `lerobot-rollout-dagger`: the `lerobot_dagger --real` session inside LeRobot's
  `lerobot-rollout`, which loads the policy in-process and connects the follower.
  Controls, recording and the dataset format are unchanged. Resets go to the
  retracted start pose of `lerobot-rollout-v2` and simulation evaluation.
  Resets and exit move both arms along the safe path in step, each from its own
  pose, with arms at up to 45 deg/s and grippers faster. `DAGGER.md` and the HG-DAgger
  lesson use it for real collection; simulation keeps `lerobot_dagger`.

- Compact `lerobot-rollout-v2` shortcut tables in the real-evaluation, co-training,
  Cosmos, and quick-reference lessons, plus a sim-versus-real success reminder
  beside the simulation benchmark.

- Opt-in Perception-DR variants for vial teleop, scripted collection, and
  evaluation. Visual-only USD sublayers supply matte foam, paper vial bodies,
  and softer light-box illumination. Per-camera highlight and grain response
  plus reset-time wrist blur are shared by display, recording, and inference.
  These tasks introduce a different image distribution; existing task IDs and
  physical geometry, calibration, and control are unchanged.

- Standalone HG-DAgger module after co-training, with a hands-on guide to
  teaching the leader rest pose, alignment, human takeovers, correction-only
  recording, and real versus simulated resets. Includes a demonstration video,
  control shortcuts, and guidance on training and autonomous evaluation.

- Robot-base tilt DR: sample -3 to +6 degrees independently per environment
  at reset, with positive tilt leaning toward the lightbox opening (world -X).
  The fixed base pivots about its nominal position; physics, robot visuals,
  and the attached wrist camera follow the pose. Applies to DR teleop,
  scripted collection, and evaluation. Non-DR tasks retain their base pose.

- Calibration checking for teleop arms with `--arm teleop`, separate leader
  statistics, and `--static-only` file checks. Both reference sets use the
  10 calibrations per arm from WowRobo robots calibrated for a live version
  of this workshop. Zero-variance reference ranges warn when the checked
  range differs.

- Scripted approach, transfer, and return curves advance by travelled distance
  with a shared cruise speed. Phase handoffs preserve target continuity;
  insertion rounds the alignment/descent corner above the rack rim. Release
  overlaps gentle opening, a 10-degree downward finger pitch, and 35 mm backward
  withdrawal, joining the return curve with a matching horizontal tangent. Both claw
  contact sensors gate retreat; unresolved releases reset after six seconds,
  and pulling the vial above the opening rejects the episode. The release
  opening is 0.52 rad. These trajectories change recorded actions;
  regenerate demonstrations for this controller.
  Release unloads the close target at the measured jaw angle and starts faster
  opening, tilt, and withdrawal together, without a fixed opening lead or
  settling delay. Transfer slows over its final 80 mm into a vertical insertion
  tangent. Return carries the incoming wrist rate through a bounded cubic
  posture curve and blends its speed over 60 mm; tighter handles reduce bowing.
  Its shared position/posture clock slows with measured Cartesian tracking
  error to reduce physical overshoot during wrist rotation.

- Scripted recording encodes accepted episodes during simulation with two
  background processes (`--encoding_workers` controls concurrency). Shutdown
  merges finished LeRobot datasets in acceptance order without re-encoding
  their videos. Collection requires fresh dataset and staging directories;
  existing output is rejected at startup instead of resumed.

- Scripted grasps descend with the jaws open at 0.4 rad until either jaw
  contacts the mat, then hold the measured arm/wrist pose and close. A
  task-local sensor filters mat contact against the two jaw bodies so contact
  with the vial cannot end descent early. The descent target advances at
  25 mm/s with a 65 mm travel bound and the six-second grasp timeout; closure
  requires an open gripper and lateral alignment. Demonstration trajectories
  change; collect fresh episodes for this controller.

- Scripted vial collection uses the real-demo 0.65 rad approach wrist posture
  and holds a -0.17 rad gripper target (about 2.1%) through grasp, transfer, and
  insertion. These controller changes come from `codex/teleop-gripper-close-hold`;
  its task-specific 0.6 N.m asset override is excluded so collection, teleop,
  and evaluation share the STS model. Grasp acceptance waits for stable contact
  and completion of the close command, allowing a held vial to block jaw travel.
  Excess force during grasp can settle within the six-second grasp timeout;
  sustained excess force during carrying still aborts. This changes scripted
  trajectories and recorded actions; regenerate demonstrations for this behavior.

- Provisional STS gripper overload protection shared by all workshop tasks.
  The USD SysID gains, passive dynamics, and 3.35 N.m peak remain the source
  parameters. The implicit gripper drive is capped at 50% of peak (1.675 N.m), then
  at 20% (0.67 N.m) after 100 ms of blocked demand above 25% of peak.
  Protection clears on reaching the requested position, a command away from
  the blocked direction, or an environment reset. These are estimated servo
  settings, not a hardware characterization. A Newton physics-step callback
  updates the Newton and MJWarp effort ceilings without replacing the implicit
  PD drive or disabling native actuators. This changes gripper dynamics;
  regenerate demonstrations and reassess existing policy performance.
  See [GRIPPER_PROTECTION.md](GRIPPER_PROTECTION.md) for assumptions and validation.

- External-camera DR: reset-time tilt of +/-5 degrees and focal length of
  97-100% of nominal, giving an equal or wider field of view. Applies to DR
  teleop, scripted collection, and evaluation; the base task and wrist camera
  retain their calibration. Multi-environment runs share one focal length,
  sampled on full resets only because tiled OVRtx projections are shared.
  Fresh DR recordings and evaluations therefore use a changed image distribution.

- Shared gripper mapping with the existing 1485-tick default and an optional
  `lerobot_eval --gripper_span_ticks` override. GR00T learning content includes
  a short example for translating measured gripper scales without retraining.

- Default measured-follower asset joint-zero override: provisional elbow flex
  -6.4 degrees. Base tilt confounds the measured shoulder difference, so shoulder
  and base orientation remain unchanged pending mounting-plane validation.
  The local workshop USDA layer applies this correction to every task while the
  upstream USD file and native-degree bridge remain unchanged. Joint coordinates
  for a given physical pose change; regenerate datasets and models for this
  configuration.

### Removed
- The unused `range_agent` sinusoidal joint-sweep command.

### Fixed
- Scripted insertion measures the vial bottom relative to its sampled grasp
  frame: the bottom is 77 mm below that frame, not 17 mm. The rack's four USD
  slot frames sit on the 73 mm opening rim instead of 27 mm above it. Insertion
  depth and obstacle clearance use these corrected coordinates, avoiding
  excessive descent and releasing after the actual tip enters the opening.
- Shared Newton/MJWarp physics uses elliptic friction cones, `impratio=10`,
  and solver tolerance `1e-10` to suppress loaded-grasp creep. Vial colliders
  use a 6 ms, critically damped normal response and higher contact impedance
  to limit jaw penetration. Their contact priority selects the vial's friction
  and contact parameters instead of mixing with the robot's soft defaults,
  reducing persistent jaw contact during release. Actuator gains, torque
  protection, and joint calibration are unchanged.
  These contact and insertion changes affect recorded trajectories and policy
  dynamics; collect fresh demonstrations and reassess existing policies.
- Scripted collection selects the nearest mat vial within 40 cm horizontally
  of the robot base that has a clear rack slot. Environments with no eligible
  vial reset after warmup, before approaching or grasping; out-of-range vials
  cannot bypass selection through a nearest-vial fallback. Rack clearance is
  also checked after grasping and during transfer/insertion. This changes target
  selection and the demonstration distribution; regenerate scripted datasets.
- Scripted rack insertion permits contact forces above 14 N while retaining
  the shared STS torque protection and transfer force limit. An insertion
  resets only its environment after three seconds without at least 0.5 mm
  improvement in measured vial-tip distance to the insertion target. Commanded
  trajectory progress does not count as physical progress. This admits
  successful placements rejected by the force guard and changes the accepted
  demonstration distribution; regenerate scripted datasets for this controller.
- Scripted vial transfers accept recovered grasps after 100 ms of renewed
  contact and allow stabilization within the existing rack-arrival timeout.
  Brief force dips do not permanently invalidate a retained vial; sustained
  contact loss still aborts the attempt. Confirmed placements tolerate up to
  8 mm of release-position tracking error before retreat, while retaining the
  settling, gripper-opening, and return-home requirements.
- **Breaking:** gripper percentage uses a 130.5495-degree calibration
  span and a fitted -12.5058-degree jaw-frame zero, in both directions, instead
  of scaling across the USD's 110-degree travel range. Sim teleop, policy
  observations/actions, and demonstration exports share this conversion.
  The workshop USD sublayer extends gripper limits to -12.5058..118.0437 degrees
  so the calibrated 0–100% interval is reachable instead of clipped at -10..100.
  At calibrated 0%, visual jaw surfaces overlap by about 1–1.5 mm near the tips;
  robot self-collision is disabled, and jaw-to-object collision remains active.
  Arm degree units and the state machine's 0.05 rad physical grasp floor are
  unchanged. Existing datasets/checkpoints encode different
  gripper coordinates and must be regenerated/retrained for this contract;
  existing artifacts are not rewritten. The 1485-tick span averages calibrations
  from 10 different WowRobo robots, replacing the initial single-follower
  1531-tick span. The jaw-frame zero retains the single-follower fit near
  12.6–27.2% opening; physical gap accuracy with the averaged span and full
  endpoint alignment remain unvalidated.
- Teleop reset-camera refresh uses the pinned renderer's scene-state updates
  without calling the unavailable cadence-reset API. Both camera images refresh
  without advancing physics before recording begins.
- Duplicate deprecated viewer settings are removed; browser disconnects no
  longer print connection-reset tracebacks.
- Local workshop USD layers rely on Isaac Lab's recursive dependency resolver
  for remote sublayers, eliminating the SO-101-specific spawn callback.
- Sequential reads of synchronized OVRtx cameras share one native render
  submission. Reset observation computation therefore retains multi-camera
  batching instead of blocking once per camera product.
- Vials farther than one metre from their environment origin terminate and reset
  only the affected environment before unbounded motion can destabilize
  simulation.
- Vial cap visual and collision height match the measured 12.77 mm reference.
  The cap top and overall vial length stay fixed. This changes rendered cap
  proportions and contact geometry; collect fresh demonstrations for this asset.
- Vial body and cap visual/collision diameters match the measured physical
  reference (28.9 mm body, 35.4 mm cap). Reset clearances include the cap's
  full radius; the bottom collider retains the tip height. This changes grasp
  contact and insertion clearance, so demonstrations must be collected again
  for the corrected geometry. Existing datasets and models are not modified.
- SO-101 arm values convert directly between LeRobot degrees and simulator
  radians without range normalization.
- Scripted vial recording pairs each observation with the post-IK, rate-limited
  joint target written for the next control interval before decimated physics.
  Capture observes the action-manager write without changing control behavior
  and preserves terminal transitions only in successful episodes.
- Scripted vial episodes open while shifting 10 mm toward the static-jaw side,
  then wait a randomized settling period. After confirmed placement, a slow
  curved retreat blends upward clearance into the homeward motion and keeps the
  gripper open through the first half of the path. Successful episodes reset
  only after returning home and closing, so demonstrations include recovery
  actions instead of ending immediately at placement.
- Teleop recording captures the current observation and owned RGB buffers before
  applying its paired leader action. Reset observations are refreshed without
  advancing physics; automatic resets discard active takes, and each take starts
  its own capture cadence. Unsupported recording rates fail instead of silently
  writing an incorrect dataset timebase. Existing datasets are not rewritten.
- Vial approaches that stop improving after their path completes reset only
  the affected environment after two seconds. Grasps without confirmed contact
  reset after six seconds, with explicit stall/timeout diagnostics.
- Vial-controller arm targets are limited after IK at each physics step, with
  braking toward the goal and authored joint limits. Cartesian and wrist
  command limiters preserve acceleration bounds when goals move or reverse.
  Sustained grasp loss aborts the transfer without waiting for rack arrival.
- State-machine slot selection checks neighboring vial geometry against the
  gripper/wrist insertion footprint in robot coordinates. A blocked descent
  holds its command and resets only the affected environment.
- All workshop environments inherit the shared Isaac Lab seed configuration.
  Entry points delegate seeding to Isaac Lab instead of setting individual RNGs;
  the state machine receives the resolved environment seed for Warp sampling.
  Policy evaluation honors the shared default unless its seed is overridden.
- Vial transfers blend orientation feedback into both wrist joints throughout
  the lift, avoiding the fixed-roll motion and abrupt correction near the rack.
- Grasp-force regulation bounds the persistent gripper target during grasp,
  transfer, and insertion to prevent accumulation outside the configured
  0.05-0.65 rad jaw range.
- Real-robot utilities import LeRobot 0.6.0's unified `so_follower` module;
  the calibration checker uses that robot's calibration directory.
- Rack safety containment now uses environment-local coordinates, preventing
  cloned state-machine environments from resetting immediately when
  `--num_envs` is greater than one.
- The vials state machine now continues regulating grasp force during transfer
  and insertion instead of freezing the gripper target after initial grasp.

### Changed
- The mat is a kinematic rigid object reset with Isaac Lab's
  `reset_root_state_uniform`. Its visual and collision poses follow the same
  native update path, retaining the base ±0.1 rad and DR ±0.3 rad yaw ranges.
  The custom mat reset and USD pose cache are removed. Seeded reset sequences
  and mat contacts change; regenerate demonstrations for this configuration.
- MJWarp line search permits up to 50 iterations for contact-heavy workshop
  steps. Converged steps exit early; difficult vial and rack contacts no longer
  flood the runtime with solver-limit diagnostics.
- Browser streaming validates the camera tensor's indexed CUDA device. A
  reusable CUDA frame buffer and stream/event synchronization hand frames to
  ovstream without host synchronization.
- Scripted vial grasps close three times faster after descent when the jaw
  midpoint is within 15 mm of the grasp target and no 1 N contact has occurred.
  First contact latches the controller back to its existing force regulation
  for the rest of the grasp; each episode clears the latch.
- The workshop image uses a multi-stage build with a CUDA 13 base runtime.
  Python packages supply their pinned CUDA libraries; compiler tooling, package
  caches, cloned repository history, upstream documentation, and upstream tests
  remain outside the runtime image.
- Isaac Lab pin advanced to develop (`1e520e9d5cb4`), including OVRtx 0.5 and
  OVStage 0.2. The workshop uses upstream per-camera render products, GPU
  hierarchy computation, and OVStage 0.2 asset-path writes while retaining its
  runtime domain-randomization mirror and ambient-fill override.
- Isaac Lab groups due cameras by renderer and submits their OVRtx products in
  one native step while retaining independent output buffers and update
  periods.
- OVRtx driver shader caches use Isaac Lab's native `OVRTX_SHADER_CACHE_PATH`
  redirection. Driver and package caches persist in separate, versioned
  directories under the mounted OVRtx cache, preserving warmed shaders across
  fresh containers without reusing incompatible data after renderer upgrades.
  Shipped cache files seed missing entries without overwriting warmed files.
- Vials-task friction, contact stiffness/damping, and MuJoCo contact settings
  use shared native material/collision configs; vial colliders retain
  `condim=6`.
- Vials author their 20 g mass with native
  `MassCfg` instead of startup events. Inertia is computed for that mass,
  reducing it to approximately 20.6% of the retained geometry-derived inertia
  and changing vial rotational dynamics.
- Vial phase goals and transitions live together, with shared command smoothing
  and centralized motion limits. Controller state and debug output omit transient
  measurements and duplicate success flags; episode acceptance uses the task's
  confirmed-placement termination.
- Vial state-machine motion rates are 20% lower, with a 25-second episode
  horizon. Each environment samples 0-3 cm of extra transfer clearance per
  episode; partial resets preserve other environments' sampled heights.
  Initial reach uses a 0.16 m/s command limit, 0.632 m/s² acceleration limit,
  and 1.24 rad/s wrist limit independently of transfer motion.
- Removed obsolete dependency compatibility paths for the controlled workshop
  container: LeRobot uses its 0.6.0 feature API, and visual reset terms use
  the pinned Isaac Lab static-asset and workshop renderer interfaces directly.
  Browser CLI settings and pynput imports use the container's required setup.
- Vial state-machine trajectories, scene geometry, contact checks, wrist DLS,
  observations, placement history, and physical reset poses use native Warp
  arrays and kernels. Torch remains at Isaac Lab manager/arm-IK interfaces
  and in recording, display, and simple command adapters. Motion limits and
  joint conventions are unchanged; Warp/NumPy sampling changes seeded random
  sequences. Grasp and placement history belongs to each environment instance.
- Initial pose configurations call Warp's built-in `quat_rpy` directly.
- Removed the unused workshop `mdp/rewards.py`; active auto-reach rewards come
  from Isaac Lab.
- Visual reset transforms use OpenUSD's `Gf.Matrix4d` quaternion conversion,
  and environment path expansion uses Isaac Lab's cloner utilities.
- Isaac Lab `develop` pin advanced to `336bfcd59d0c` for the SO-101 SysID asset
  (isaac-sim/IsaacLab#7483). OVRtx `0.4.1.364340` and OVStage `0.1.1.355824`
  are unchanged. The range also turns OVRtx shadows off by default in RTX
  Minimal mode, which does not affect the workshop's path-traced `rgb` cameras.
- **Breaking:** the robot now spawns the public
  `Robots_Multiphysics/RobotStudio/so101_new_calib_SysID/so101_new_calib.usda`
  asset, replacing the temporary `isaac-dev` URI, and resolves its actuator
  model from the `physics` variant instead of the workshop's per-joint gains.
  That variant authors the AnchorBench system-identification fit: effort limit
  3.35 N·m, velocity limit 30 rad/s, per-joint stiffness 14.7-68.3 (was 10-33),
  and per-joint damping, armature, friction, dynamic friction, and viscous
  friction that were previously unset. Prim
  paths, joint limits, masses, inertias, and joint ordering are unchanged, so
  the authored joint ranges and `SO101_JOINT_ORDER` still hold — but the plant
  is different. **Datasets recorded and policies fine-tuned before this
  change are off-distribution and should be re-recorded and retrained.**
- `soft_joint_pos_limit_factor` is pinned to `1.0`. Upstream moved its default
  to `0.98`, which would clamp the authored rest pose (`shoulder_lift` sits on
  its stop by design) and shrink every teleop range by 2%.
- The SysID asset resolves ~15 authored convex-hull collision proxies on the
  robot that a broken reference path previously suppressed. Contact count is
  higher; `nconmax=512` and the grasp thresholds need re-validation.
- Rack SDF cooking now uses Isaac Lab's
  `NewtonSDFCollisionPropertiesCfg` instead of a workshop MODEL_INIT hook.
  Newton handles the rack's open mesh through its winding-number SDF path, so
  the local trimesh repair pipeline and mesh-approximation override API were
  removed.
- Vial-task contact stiffness, damping, friction, and MuJoCo impedance remain
  scoped to the vial, rack, mat, and lightbox. The robot retains its
  USD-authored materials and instanceable geometry.
- Renamed the wrist and external camera entities, observations, browser feeds,
  and LeRobot features from `ego` / `external_D455` to `wrist` / `external`.
  New datasets therefore use `observation.images.wrist` and
  `observation.images.external`; older checkpoints require the eval rename map
  `{"wrist":"ego","external":"external_D455"}`.

## 30-08-26 /1

### Changed
- Isaac Lab `develop` pin advanced to `4f154633e603` and now supplies the
  compatible OVRtx `0.4.1.364340` and OVStage `0.1.1.355824` packages.
- OVRtx scene ownership and cloning now use OVStage. Runtime light, transform,
  and HDRI domain-randomization writes target every clone-plan environment and
  every active renderer backend through OVStage.
- Ego and external cameras now share one native OVRtx backend. The renderer
  batches one stable tiled render product per camera in a single native step,
  preserving independent projections without the two-backend first-frame
  deadlock or per-frame relationship rebuilds.
- Camera configuration now selects the workshop renderer directly through
  `OVRTXRendererCfg.class_type`. `utils/ovrtx` holds the renderer
  (`renderer.py`) and its OVStage attribute writes (`ovstage_writes.py`).
- `SO101OVRTXRenderer` sets `ISAAC_LAB_OVRTX_USE_OVSTAGE=1` and
  `ISAAC_LAB_OVRTX_READ_GPU_TRANSFORMS=0` itself, so the backend flags it
  depends on no longer have to be supplied by the container environment.
- Each camera render product now authors `deviceIds` from the Warp device, so
  its render-var buffers are allocated where the consuming kernels run.
- The OVRtx step advances by the camera update period rather than a fixed
  60 Hz, matching the 30 Hz cadence the workshop tasks actually render at.
  Cameras sharing the renderer must now agree on `update_period`.
- Per-tile DLSS history resets on environment reset were dropped after visual
  inspection showed no pre-reset bleed without them.
- Browser streaming and its static client assets now live together under
  `utils/ovstream`. The display is WebRTC-only; the CPU-readback MJPEG
  fallback and its duplicate browser path were removed.
- Robot-color domain randomization now uses Isaac Lab's renderer-native
  `VisualMaterialCfg` pipeline for the printed SO-101 materials, while the
  motor and rack materials are registered to preserve their authored look
  across OVStage clones.
- Camera observations and fixed vial masses now use Isaac Lab's built-in
  `image` and `randomize_rigid_body_mass` terms. Unused camera DR terms and
  redundant shared-renderer cleanup bookkeeping were removed.

### Fixed
- Environment-local mat, camera, and light transforms no longer reset their
  inherited transform stack.
- Reset-time mat rotation now honors the resetting environment IDs instead of
  moving every cloned mat when any one environment resets.
- Runtime HDRI swaps recreate the OVStage asset column with `ASSET_STRING`
  values, so texture changes reach detached renderer stages. The global dome
  light changes only on full-scene resets, not when one environment resets.
- Light-box exposure randomization is scoped to the resetting environments.
- Robot material randomization applies one color consistently to all printed
  materials in each environment.
- Shared camera rendering publishes pending visual-material writes before the
  native render step, preventing fallback-yellow robot materials.
- Shared camera rendering disables OVRtx GPU transform reads, preventing
  geometry streaming from corrupting one of the camera products.
- SO-101 collision meshes use convex hulls instead of the asset's authored
  convex decomposition, avoiding thousands of unnecessary robot collision
  pieces while preserving the rack SDF and primitive vial colliders. The
  reduced contact load allows `nconmax` to decrease from 1024 to 512.
- The vials state machine fills rear rack slots before front slots and masks a
  rear slot when its paired front slot is occupied, avoiding obstructed
  lowering paths through an existing vial.

## 16-08-26 /1

### Fixed
- Newton collision capacity now auto-sizes with the configured environment
  count instead of failing when multi-environment contact limits exceed the
  previous single-environment allocation.
- Light-box walls/base and the mat now use separate `visual` and `collision`
  meshes so static surfaces appear in the Newton visualizer. Collision meshes
  use USD guide purpose and remain excluded from OVRtx rendering.

### Changed
- Environment spacing reduced from 4 m to 1 m for a more compact multi-env
  layout.

## 15-08-26 /1

### Changed
- Robot configuration now derives from Isaac Lab's `SO101_CFG` and loads the
  multiphysics `so101_new_calib.usda` asset with explicit `Robot=robot`,
  `Sensor=sensors`, and `Physics=physics` variants.
- Simulation joint names now match the canonical LeRobot names
  (`shoulder_pan` through `gripper`); the asset's authored articulation order
  matches the existing dataset and checkpoint feature order.
- The ego camera now binds to the calibrated `wowrobo_2MP_camera` authored by
  the asset's sensor variant instead of duplicating its pose and OpenCV
  intrinsics in Python.
- Workshop-specific measured rest pose, per-joint actuator tuning, Newton
  contact settings, moving-jaw contact sensing, and robot-color DR are
  preserved on the upstream asset.
- The upstream adapter's post-spawn callback normalizes the authored ego-camera
  transform scale and de-instances nested visual/material references in the
  in-memory stage root layer. Isaac Lab cloning preserves those root-layer
  opinions without requiring renderer-stage preparation.
- The ego camera's previous `(0.001, 5.0)` clipping range is restored to avoid
  slicing nearby grasped objects, and robot-color updates now include the
  sensor payload's separate printed-plastic material so the camera mount
  matches the rest of the arm.
- The external task view now uses a standalone pinhole camera with the D455
  pose and workshop optics. Loading the public Isaac 6.0 D455 asset caused
  delayed dynamic-geometry corruption in OVRtx even when rendering through a
  separate camera, so the unused sensor body is intentionally not composed.
- Repository guidance now uses the standard `AGENTS.md` filename, and camera
  descriptions consistently identify the standalone external camera.

### Removed
- Standalone local SO-101 definition and `SO-ARM101-USD-Newton-CH.usd`,
  superseded by the Isaac Lab-derived adapter and multiphysics asset.
- Vendored `assets/usd/sensors/rsd455.usd`.

## 14-08-26 /1

### Changed
- Isaac Lab `develop` pin advanced to `c8876fc81a8a` with ovrtx 0.4.x.
- Camera commands no longer use the removed ovrtx 0.3 `libcarb.so`
  `LD_PRELOAD` workaround.
- Light-box RectLight dimensions are authored with explicit USD `width` and
  `height` attributes instead of transform scale, matching the original Isaac
  Sim setup and avoiding renderer-version-dependent light sizing.
- Light-box RectLight exposure is calibrated to `2.0` (with intensity `100`) to
  preserve the ovrtx 0.3 workshop brightness after ovrtx 0.4 corrected
  transform-derived RectLight sizing; DR tasks randomize its exposure over
  `(-2.0, 2.0)`.
- Ego-camera calibration now uses Isaac Lab's native
  `OpenCvPinholeDistortionCfg` while preserving the existing intrinsics and
  distortion coefficients.
- The workshop OVRtx renderer patch now delegates scene initialization and
  camera updates to the current upstream implementation while retaining the
  per-camera backend, ambient-fill, multi-backend partition, and DR-mirroring
  behavior still specific to this workshop.
- Sky-light DR once again samples the bundled HDRIs and mirrors runtime
  asset-path changes into every OVRtx camera backend.

### Removed
- Private `OpenCvPinholeCameraCfg` spawner, superseded by Isaac Lab #6608.

## 15-07-26 /2

### Changed
- OVRtx renderer patch simplified to one render product per camera and renamed
  `MultiCamOVRTXRenderer` → `SO101OVRTXRenderer` (module
  `utils/ovrtx_multicam.py` → `utils/so101_ovrtx_renderer.py`, env var
  `NO_MULTICAM` → `NO_OVRTX_PATCH`). Dropped the dead multi-camera tiling path
  (offset kernels, tile-offset / global-cols machinery, cross-camera frame
  cache) now that each camera binds its own backend, and folded in upstream's
  raise-instead-of-swallow behavior on render/binding failures. Kept the
  ambient-fill fix, deferred DR write-queue, and multi-backend scene-partition
  guard.

### Removed
- `tools/ovrtx_multicam_freeze_repro.py` — the tiled multi-camera freeze it
  guarded no longer exists (one render product per camera).

## 15-07-26 /1

### Changed
- Ego (gripper) camera now uses the calibrated OpenCV pinhole model of the real
  SO-101 wrist camera instead of a plain USD pinhole. The projection is authored
  as an `OmniLensDistortionOpenCvPinholeAPI` on the camera prim — OVRtx honors it
  natively, so `fx/fy/cx/cy` + `k1/k2/k3/p1/p2` distortion drive the render — and
  the ego camera pose was retuned to match. Changes the recorded ego RGB, so
  datasets and policies from the old pinhole no longer match.

### Added
- `sim/opencv_pinhole_camera.py`: `OpenCvPinholeCameraCfg` + spawner that layers
  the OpenCV lens-distortion API onto a pinhole camera; `apply_lens_distortion`
  toggles the distortion coefficients.

## 14-07-26 /1

Bulk summary of the sim-to-real end-to-end work landed since the develop-pin
migration (record → push → GR00T fine-tune → eval, in sim and on the real robot).

### Added
- GR00T policy evaluation in sim: `Newton-So101-Teleop-Vials-To-Rack-Eval-v0`
  / `-DR-Eval-v0` tasks with success termination, run against a remote GR00T
  policy server via `lerobot_eval`.
- LeRobot dataset recording ported to the Newton module (RGB video + joint
  state/action) with `--repo_id` / `--repo_root` / `--task_name` and Hub push.
- Browser sensor display over ovstream WebRTC (`--browser-display`): live camera
  video, rolling joint/action plots, per-camera FPS, a recording indicator, and
  a "saving — do not close" guard.
- Real-robot + GR00T inference Docker container, with a bundled policy server.
- Per-camera OVRtx FOV (one render product per camera) and external-camera
  orientation domain randomization.

### Changed
- Renamed the workshop package `sim_to_real_so101_newton` → `sim_to_real_so101`
  and de-suffixed the console scripts (`list_envs`, `zero_agent`, `random_agent`,
  `range_agent`, `lerobot_agent`, `lerobot_eval`).
- Sim Docker image migrated to the Kit-less Newton stack; installation
  streamlined and bumped to LeRobot 0.6.0.
- `.dockerignore` now excludes the runtime-mounted `datasets/`, `outputs/`, and
  `models/` directories from the Docker build context.

### Removed
- PhysX support: the physics preset now defaults to Newton (no `physx` preset),
  and PhysX-only configuration was dropped.
- Debug / diagnostic envs and their `tools/` monitors (the Cartpole build-proof
  and the vial physics-isolation harnesses).
- GLFW sensor display, superseded by the browser display.

## 21-06-26 /2

### Fixed
- OVRtx light-box exposure regression: `isaaclab_ov`'s render scope baked a
  full-strength ambient fill (`omni:rtx:rt:ambientLight:intensity = 1.0`) that
  lit the whole scene independently of the authored lights and blew out the
  light-box. `MultiCamOVRTXRenderer` now zeroes that attribute in the generated
  render-scope USD, so illumination comes only from the authored RectLight.

### Changed
- Light-box asset switched from binary `lightbox-simple.usd` to re-authored
  text `lightbox-simple.usda`.
- Light-box `randomize_light_exposure` DR is now a no-op range `(0, 0)` (the
  earlier ~10x dial-down is no longer needed after the ambient-light fix);
  sky-light exposure DR range retuned to `(-3.0, 3.0)`.

## 21-06-26 /1

### Changed
- Newton port now targets Isaac Lab `develop` (pinned at `01de15e155`) with
  ovrtx 0.3.0, using the develop `launch_simulation` API.

### Fixed
- Robot link visuals are de-instanced in the SO-ARM101 USD so the moving arm
  renders correctly in the OVRtx cameras (ovrtx 0.3.0 does not transform
  USD-instanced geometry at runtime).
- `list_envs` and the `Newton-Vial-Drop-Only-v0` env no longer crash on
  the Kit-less develop runtime.

